characterized samples  in which the variant allele fraction is  already known  the next item from the molecular  pathology checklist optical -  next-generation sequencing based testing  is a requirement to establish the  analytical specificity for the testing  and as previously noted analytical  specificity is the ability of a test to  correctly identify or quantify an entity  in the presence of interfering or cross  reacting substances that might expect it  to be present  we know that interfering or cross  reactive substances might be specimen or  tissue type dependent such as the  hemoglobin that's present in blood  specimens melanin that might be  associated with melanoma samples or  other substances that may be associated  with some samples such as lipids mucin  heavy metals in fixation methods or even  contaminating cells  specimen carryover can also be a problem  with next-generation sequencing based  tests especially those that are designed  to detect variants with low allele  frequencies  an item from the all common checklist  vil to next generation sequencing based  testing requires that any clinical  claims made by the laboratory must be  validated for all laboratory developed  tests some examples of clinical claims  might include statements about the  diagnostic sensitivity and specificity  of the testing the ability to predict  risk of disease it might be statements  relative to the clinical usefulness of a  test or even its cost-effectiveness  checklist item also states that the  clinical laboratory must perform a  clinical validation study to support any  of the clinical claims that they make  and this validation requires a minimum  of 20 samples  another item from the molecular  pathology checklist requires that the  clinical performance characteristics of  each assay are determined and recorded  either using literature citations or a  summary of internal study results  the clinical performance  characteristics of a test basically  relate to the diagnostic sensitivity and  specificity of the testing positive and  negative predictive values in the target  population for the testing and also the  clinical utility of the testing  the next item from the molecular  pathology checklist  regarding next generation sequencing wet  bench validation requires that all next  generation sequencing laboratories  validate their analytical wet bench  processes and then revalidate these  processes when modifications are made  the analytical wet bench process and  bioinformatics processes for any given  test will likely occur within a single  laboratory but in today's world we know  that a combination of primary and  referral laboratories may also be  present with the wet bench process for  example performed in the primary  laboratory and then the laboratory  referring its bioinformatics analysis to  another company however even if these  two processes occur in different  laboratories the validation of both the  wet bench and bioinformatics processes  must be integrated to ensure that there  is acceptable beginning to end  performance for the whole pipeline  analytical wet bench validations must  also include a method space validation  to establish  general performance for detection of the  sequence variant types that the assay is  designed to identify  and the validation must also establish  metrics and quality control parameters  that will be used to assess the wet  bench process performance  it's important to note that it's the  primary responsibility of the laboratory  director to review and approve all  validations that are relevant to the  intended tests for the processes  performed in their laboratory as well as  for any processes that might be  performed in referral laboratories if  applicable  another important item from the  molecular pathology checklist applicable  to next generation sequencing testing  requires that all next generation  sequencing laboratories also validate  their analytical bioinformatics process  and then revalidate these processes when  any modifications are made  analytical wet bench process and  bioinformatics processes for a test  might occur again within a single  laboratory as is done in most cases or  in a combination of primary and referral  laboratories however the validation of  both of these processes the wet bench  and bioinformatics processes must be  integrated to ensure beginning to end  test performance  a method space approach can also be used  for the analytical bioinformatics  process validation using a sufficiently  high number of variants that the test is  designed to detect such as single  nucleotide variants insertions and  deletions copy number variants as well  as other structural variants such as  translocations and inversions  as with the wet bench process metrics  and quality control parameters must also  be established to assess the  bioinformatics process performance  during the validation and again it's the  primary responsibility of the laboratory  director to review and approve all of  these bioinformatics process validations  and also by informatics processes that  might be performed in possible referral  laboratories  this next  next generation sequencing related item  from the molecular pathology checklist  requires that there must be an actual  validation summary document that  addresses all the analytical and  clinical performance parameters of the  testing  and of course the laboratory director  must review and approve this validation  summary prior to starting any clinical  testing and that's a very important  point that is overlooked by some  laboratories that any testing that they  perform must be approved by their  laboratory director prior to any  clinical testing being performed by the  laboratory  just shows  some of the  detailed steps that should be included  in a good validation summary  so you need a detailed step-by-step  description of the assay and how it will  be implemented  clinical indications and the intended  use of the assay should also be included  in this summary the  the name of the assay and any  instruments that are used all methods  and reagents required for the assay  should be a part of this summary the FDA  status or ldt designation of the testing  a very important part of the validation  is all the software scripts and software  version numbers in the adviser Mattox  pipeline and a good practice for any  laboratory using a pipeline is to name  each version of their pipeline from  beginning to end so that it could be  reproduced if needed all specimen types  and numbers of specimens should be  listed in the validation summary all the  analytical performance and clinical  performance characteristics should be  present in equality control and quality  assurance metrics to ensure that the  testing is being done properly should be  in the validation summary and there  should be a description also any  required proficiency testing which could  come from the College of American  Pathologists or it might be an  alternative proficiency testing method  with a detailed description and  acceptable criteria for the testing any  relevant safety issues related to the  testing should be listed you should  document notification of the CA P if  you're an accredited CA P laboratory  that you're initiating a new test and  good practice also requires a statement  like I've listed here this validation  study has been reviewed and the  performance of the method is considered  acceptable for patient testing and there  should be a section in the validation  summary for the laboratories directors  signature with date and also the date  that the actual patient testing began  which of course should be some time  either the day that the laboratory  director signs or some time after that  so what are the specimen types that  should be used for a next-generation  sequencing validation the molecular  checklist item mo l dot three one zero  one five requires that the validation  must include representative sample types  that are expected to be tested by the  assay including such specimen types such  as peripheral blood plasma bone marrow  specimens saliva specimens other body  fluids fresh tissue or fresh frozen  tissue formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded  tissue samples buccal swabs or perhaps  even prenatal specimens previously  characterized clinical samples with each  type of pathogenic alteration that the  assay is intended to detect should also  be used so if your assay is designed to  detect single nucleotide variants for  example those should examples of those  samples should be included and if the  assay is designed to detect insertions  deletions structural variants or copy  number variants there should be examples  in your validation of all those type  variants  other validation materials that could  also be used include proficiency testing  materials and a very useful category  that we use actively in in my laboratory  for testing validation is in silikal  generated datasets for bioinformatics  analysis and these are basically  sequence files that were obtained from  next-generation sequencing analysis of  well characterized reference specimen  that have then been manipulated by  computerized algorithms in order to  introduce sequence variants at various  allele frequencies for subsequent  analysis and detection so these are  extremely these types of datasets are  extremely useful for testing the  bioinformatics portion of your  next-generation sequencing pipeline  commercially available reference  materials may also be used for  next-generation sequencing validations  and there are many of these available I  didn't list any specific companies but  they're pretty easy to find and we've  used many of these in my own laboratory  for validation purposes you can use a  very well characterized human genome  reference standard the NIS t cell line  na12878 there are many commercially  available reference genomic DNA  standards that have different types of  variants at known variant frequencies  including very low allele frequencies to  help you test your lower limit of  detection in your assay and there are  also a number of commercially available  reference FFPE specimen standards with  known allele frequencies and variant  types that you can use if your assay is  designed to detect variants and FFPE  tissues  so a very common question that that I  get and the c.a.p gets is how many  specimens should I use for my next  generation sequencing validation the all  common checklist item Co m dot 403 5-0  requires a minimum of 20 samples for any  test validation and there are really no  other number limitations stated in the  checklist requirements and this leaves  the actual number of specimens used for  the validation at the discretion of the  laboratory director and the validation  summary should justify whatever number  of specimens are chosen for the  validation and of course  the validation should also include  previously characterized samples of the  specimen type that are intended to be  used for the assay as well as specimens  with pathogenic alterations that are  intended to be detected by the assay a  recent publication from Jennings at all  in the Journal of molecular diagnostics  I have the the reference listed on the  slide the bottom part of the slide  recommends using a minimum of 59 samples  for validation of a next-generation  sequencing Oncology panel and today I  think this is actually a pretty  reasonable recommendation in a recent  validation that we performed of 425 gene  panel we used over a hundred validation  samples and I think this is really  reasonable today because there are so  many clinical specimens available from  other laboratories performing  next-generation sequencing that you can  obtain a number of commercially  available reference standards as well as  as I mentioned the very useful NIS  t-cell line in a one two eight seven  eight  this slide contains a number of next  generation sequencing validation  articles from a recent issue of the  archives of pathology and Laboratory  Medicine and all these articles contain  useful information regarding  next-generation sequencing  testing validation and you can use those  as you see fit  another good recent manuscript from dr.  Abney Santana and her colleagues was  published in the may 2019 issue the  Journal of molecular diagnostics as  noted here on the slide and this this  manuscript contains many practical  step-by-step guidance instructions for  next generation sequencing based testing  validations and finally additional  helpful next generation sequencing  laboratory worksheets are also available  from the CA P public website that  discusses various topics such as test  content design test optimization test  validation quality management for the  laboratory as well as bioinformatics  processes in the lab  that is my last slide and I'll be happy  to entertain any questions if there are  some thank you thank you dr. repertory  for your informative presentation we  will now start the Q&amp;A portion of the  webinar if you have a question you'd  like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask the question box  located on the far left of your screen  well answer as many questions as we have  time for okay let's get started  our first question is is a revalidation  of our whole exome sequencing  bioinformatics pipeline required if our  bioinformatics pipeline is updated to a  new version  answer is yes so anytime any  any changes are made to either the wet  bench process or the bioinformatics  pipeline the assay should be revalidated  from beginning to end to ensure that  testing accuracy is maintained even in  the context of the changes or change or  changes that were made to the testing  methodology that's a very important  requirement and any good laboratory  should be revalidating their process any  time any changes are made either to the  wet bench analytical process or the blen  firm Attucks pipeline  you  great thank you okay next question must  our NGS validation studies include all  possible mutations detected by our  analysis of gene regions that we  routinely analyzed and report out  but of course it's not realistic and and  most likely not even possible for most  generation sequencing validation studies  to include all the possible genetic  alterations that might be expected to be  detected by the testing so in this  context as mentioned before in the  presentation a methods based validation  approach is completely acceptable as  long as it includes the types of  specimens that are expected to be tested  in the assay as well as the categories  of variant types that are expected to be  tested by the assay  thank you again dr. Robert Horry for  your time today and more important  research we would also like to thank lab  roots and our sponsor Thermo Fisher  Scientific for underrating underwriting  today's educational webcast before we go  i'd like to thank the audience for  joining us today and for their questions  any questions we did not have time for  today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration this webcast  can be viewed on demand lab routes will  alert you via email when it's available  for replay we encourage you to share  that email with your colleagues who may  have missed today's event until next  time device
 

